so for those who don't know we wrote our  first book and it finally comes out next  week On January 15 when I say we I mean  a good friend of mine who runs a YouTube  channel legends of history who is a very  talented writer and myself now the  purpose of this video is to go with the  book in some more detail so those who  are new to the channel and those who may  be on the fence wanting some more  information before they purchase the  book now have that information to start  the book is called Greek mythology  explained which has definitely caused  some confusion as some people took that  to mean that the book would be a deep  analytical dive into Greek mythology  itself which isn't really what we were  going for the title more so relates to  the channel name there are still  explanations of each story historically  in contextually with an insight into  what we were thinking as writers but the  main focus of the book is the retelling  of six stories that we've chosen  almost like an anthology these stories  are Odysseus and he's encounter with  Scylla the story of Lamia and how she  became a monster the rise and fall of  Bellerophon the events lead into  Medusa's transformation the fall of  Icarus and the tragic love story of  Artemis and Orion each one of these  stories is illustrated and then  accompanied by a brief explanation  portion so why exactly did we choose to  structure the book in this way for me  personally one thing that I've always  felt has been missing from these old  texts and books is immersion the works  of Homer  Hesiod Ovid and many other poets of the  time often feel like a summary of a  story when compared to the standards of  writing that we expect today at times it  can be quite hard to write to their  characters especially those we would  consider outside of the major figures  because they lack personality and that's  because the level of detail just isn't  there what we've tried to do is stay  true to these stories but also fill in  the huge gaps and give these characters  personalities motives aspirations fears  and an overall reason for the audience  to relate to them we've turned these  brief summaries and timelines into full  length coherent stories I think one of  the main reasons that more people don't  enjoy mythology is the fact that many of  these current texts only appeal to those  who already have an interest in  mythology now don't get me wrong these  texts and books are a great source of  information and historical context but  if it's not something you're interested  in then the likes of Homer's Iliad and  Hesiod's Theogony are never really going  to capture your imagination and emerge  you into a story it's much easier to  take an interest in a character when you  I must into a story as opposed to just  reading the list of things that they may  or may not have done whether you like or  dislike postie Jackson and Rick  Riordan's books they have a clear goal  and they certainly achieve that goal  they act as a gateway into mythology for  a younger audience and I know many  people who now love mythology because of  those books and that is what we've set  out to do of this book we wanted to  create this gateway into mythology for a  slightly older audience but also wanted  to create a book that appealed to the  already existed mythology fans present  in these stories that they most likely  would have heard of before but in a new  and exciting way shining new light on  characters they may have read about in  passing but don't really know you have  characters like medusa Bellerophon and  Lamia where they have these very  interesting stories but even in their  own myths and legends they seldom feel  like the main character which is why  we've told these stories from their  perspective because for the most part  the story does remain true to the source  material but the way you feel about the  story and the characters certainly  changes especially since so many of  these stories feel like a book in  themselves once you really delve into  them now as for the overall tone of the  book it's definitely not one that's for  children if at one end of the spectrum  you have Percy Jackson and at the other  you have a game the throne shall we say  we certainly lean more towards Game of  Thrones and that's because these stories  are an unfiltered retelling of Greek  mythology there's murder betrayal  revenge adventure jealousy love and sex  and pretty much everything that you  would expect from Greek mythology each  story is also illustrated by a different  artist which I feel does help set a  different tone but still ties the book  nicely together most of the art that  you've seen over the course of this  video will be in the book to summarize I  feel the book is an interest in and  unique read for all audiences whether  you love mythology or whether it's new  to you we are definitely looking forward  to seeing how you as an audience write  to the book and how you feel about our  interpretations and the overall  structure there will be a few live  streams in February where we discuss the  book almost like an online book club  where everyone can come in and share  their thoughts and ideas and we can get  your feedback to answer the last  question that I see asked quite often  will there be more books like this and  will they be about other mythologies to  be honest as of right now we don't  really know and we do have two more  books of our publisher but we haven't  really decided what they'll be yet so I  guess it depends on the success of the  first book  we are however working on our own  separate projects such as the dark world  series which most likely will be  published as a book and an audiobook  once it's finished so if you haven't  already checked that out then the first  episode Krampus night is up on the  channel and it was definitely a fun one  to record we are also working on our  teen slash young adult mythology based  series which is probably will all most  excited about right now and hopefully  book 1 will be ready for the summer but  we'll definitely keep you posted on that  one on a quick side note I'd like to  thank everyone for a great year last  year it's because of you that we have  the opportunity to write these books and  work on these projects and we never  wanted to create something that we  wouldn't read ourselves and I honestly  believe that you will love Greek  mythology explained and the future  projects that we have coming for those  who haven't ordered the book yet you can  get the Kindle when the hardcover  versions on Amazon and most major book  retailers the B link in the description  for those in the United States and those  outside can simply order it from their  countries Amazon page as always I've  been your host mythology and fiction  explained but before we do part ways  I'll leave you with the trailer for the  book for those who haven't watched it  yet or for those who would like to watch  it again  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

